/// =====================================================================================
/// dim_currency — Type 1, and the home of the minor-units contract
///
/// `minor_unit_digits` is the column that makes every money figure in this repo correct, and it
/// is **not** used by any measure in this model. That is deliberate and worth being explicit
/// about, because it looks like an omission:
///
///   * `amount_minor` is in the transaction's own currency, so summing it across currencies is
///     meaningless — 100 JPY and 100 GBP are not 200 of anything. No measure sums it.
///   * `amount_gbp_minor` is the reporting-currency conversion, and GBP has exactly 2 minor-unit
///     digits, always. So every money measure divides the minor-unit sum by a literal 100 —
///     `DIVIDE(SUM(...), 100)` — and needs no lookup at all.
///
/// **Where that division lives is not a free choice**, and getting it wrong is silent, so it is
/// spelled out here once for the whole model.
///
/// It cannot live in the format string. A DAX format string formats; the only scaling it can
/// express is the trailing comma's thousands, and there is no /100. `\£#,0.00` applied to 12345
/// minor units renders `£12,345.00`, not `£123.45` — the same figure, a hundred times too large,
/// with a currency symbol in front of it making it look considered.
///
/// It cannot live in a calculated column either, which is the tidy answer in an Import model. This
/// is Direct Lake: the model's columns *are* the warehouse's columns, and a calculated column would
/// break the Direct Lake contract (`test_there_are_no_calculated_columns` holds that line).
///
/// So it lives in the measure, and the rule that follows is worth stating as a rule: **a `_minor`
/// column is formatted as an integer (`#,0`), and a £ sign appears only on a measure.** Minor units
/// are integers everywhere this model stores them — that is the whole reason the warehouse stores
/// them, since integer pence are exact and a decimal pound column accumulates float error over a
/// sum of millions of rows — and pounds exist only where a measure has converted them.
///
/// How this was found, because it is the more useful half of the story: every money measure in this
/// model *did* carry a money format string over a minor-unit sum, and this note *did* claim the
/// format string divided by 100. Nothing caught it. Twenty-three tests check that the model is
/// internally consistent and consistent with the warehouse DDL, and all of them passed, because a
/// format string that formats the wrong magnitude is consistent with everything. What caught it was
/// `dashboard/build_dashboard.py` the first time it ran: it renders each measure through the format
/// string the TMDL gives it and applies no scaling of its own, and it printed an average approved
/// card payment of £6,379.00. The truth was £63.79. A test that checks a claim beats a comment that
/// makes one, and a number you can look at beats both.
///
/// The scaling that `minor_unit_digits` drives — `10^(2 - digits)`, which is what makes JPY
/// (0 digits) convert correctly — happens **once**, in silver, before the fact is written. By
/// the time data reaches this model the problem is already solved, and a measure that consulted
/// this column would be solving it a second time and disagreeing with the warehouse.
///
/// The column stays visible anyway, because "why is this JPY figure what it is" is a question
/// someone will ask of a report, and the answer is in this column.
/// =====================================================================================
table dim_currency
	lineageTag: c98af7e1-c081-5453-b8b0-f1ba7e7dfff9

	column currency_sk
		dataType: int64
		isHidden
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: currency_sk

	column currency_code
		dataType: string
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: currency_code

	column currency_name
		dataType: string
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: currency_name

	column minor_unit_digits
		dataType: int64
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: minor_unit_digits

	column is_reporting_currency
		dataType: boolean
		summarizeBy: none
		sourceColumn: is_reporting_currency

	partition dim_currency = entity
		mode: directLake
		source
			entityName: dim_currency
			schemaName: dbo
			expressionSource: DatabaseQuery
